# developer-team ACTOR tenant grant — the READ half of 06 §2.2's developer-team template, joined
# with 06 §2.2.1's broker-operations grant. THE INSTALL-PATH COPY.
#
# Rendered by `render_actor_grant developer-team <namespace> <leaf>` in common.sh and applied by the
# step that installs the tier, before the identity that binds to it.
#
# ── WHY A ROLE AND NEVER A CLUSTERROLE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 06 §2.2 spells this tier's template `kind: Role` in its one namespace, and `vap-agent-readonly`
# validation 2 enforces it from the other side: a ClusterRole labelled `kube-agents/tier:
# developer-team` is denied at admission as a wrong-scope grant (03 §4). So this file has one Role
# and no cluster-scoped object at all — and that is also why the tier-neutral
# `kubeagents-broker-operations` ClusterRole in broker-operations-grant.yaml.template SURVIVES this
# unit rather than being retired with its namespaced twin. Three of §2.2.1's ten cluster-scoped
# triples (`tokenreviews`, `fleetfreezes`, `changepolicies`) address cluster-scoped kinds that a
# namespaced Role cannot grant, and 06 §2.2.1 says losing the `fleetfreezes` read does not fail safe
# — it bricks the tier (06 §4.4). A namespace-scoped tier therefore needs a cluster-scoped object it
# does not own, and the only one that is admissible is one wearing no tier label at all.
#
# They are still written out below, inert, because 06 §2.2.1 appends the grant "verbatim to every
# actor Role/ClusterRole" and V-BRK-013 property 4 holds the union of this tier's objects to exactly
# that. An inert rule grants nothing; a missing one would make the spec and the tree disagree about
# what the tier is owed, which is the drift the check exists to refuse.
#
# ── WHAT IS ABSENT, AND WHY THE ABSENCE IS THE POINT ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Every WRITE verb of 06 §2.2. Phase 9 renders the read half only: `create`, `update`, `patch`,
# `delete` and the `pods/eviction` rule are specified, unrendered, and — while the three copies of
# `vap-agent-readonly` carry the read disjunct and nothing wider — inadmissible. The write half
# arrives with `vap-agent-scope` in P10-T1, in the same unit that widens the policy to the per-tier
# allow-list 03 §4.2 requires. `DARK_PROFILE` in dev/tests/actor-grant-single-sourced.py is the
# switch, and it is one switch for both halves on purpose: flipping either alone turns V-BRK-013 red.
#
# The `deployments/scale` rule keeps `get` and loses `update`/`patch`, which is what "the READ verbs
# of the template" means applied rule by rule rather than rule-wise: a rule is not dropped because it
# holds a write verb, it is narrowed to the read verbs it holds.
#
# ── NO `kube-agents/scope` LABEL ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 06 §2's identity table lists one; it is deliberately absent here for the reason recorded at length
# in examples/gitops-repo/policy/rbac-overlay/platform.yaml — the label's value is
# `agentlabels.RenderScope`, not the scope leaf in the SA name, and writing it by hand would create a
# second definition site for a value that already has one in Go.
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: ${AGENT_ACTOR_KSA}
  namespace: ${AGENT_NAMESPACE}
  labels:
    kube-agents/tier: developer-team
    kube-agents/role: actor
rules:
  # ── The READ half of 06 §2.2, developer-team ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods", "services", "configmaps", "secrets", "serviceaccounts", "persistentvolumeclaims", "endpoints"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods/log", "pods/status", "events"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: ["apps"]
    resources: ["deployments", "statefulsets", "daemonsets", "replicasets"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  # `get` only. The template grants get/update/patch here; the two writes are the write half.
  - apiGroups: ["apps"]
    resources: ["deployments/scale", "statefulsets/scale", "replicasets/scale"]
    verbs: ["get"]
  - apiGroups: ["batch"]
    resources: ["jobs", "cronjobs"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: ["autoscaling"]
    resources: ["horizontalpodautoscalers"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: ["policy"]
    resources: ["poddisruptionbudgets"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: ["networking.k8s.io"]
    resources: ["ingresses", "networkpolicies"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: ["gateway.networking.k8s.io"]
    resources: ["httproutes", "grpcroutes"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  # ── 06 §2.2.1, appended verbatim ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # The first three rules are inert in a namespaced Role — see the header. They are here because the
  # spec appends the grant to every actor Role, not to every actor ClusterRole.
  - apiGroups: ["authentication.k8s.io"]
    resources: ["tokenreviews"]
    verbs: ["create"]
  - apiGroups: ["kubeagents.x-k8s.io"]
    resources: ["fleetfreezes"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: ["kubeagents.x-k8s.io"]
    resources: ["changepolicies"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  # These four are the effective half, and the only two verbs in the whole grant that change stored
  # state (`create actionrecords`, the status update) are bound in this namespace and nowhere else.
  - apiGroups: ["kubeagents.x-k8s.io"]
    resources: ["actionrecords"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "create"]
  - apiGroups: ["kubeagents.x-k8s.io"]
    resources: ["actionrecords/status"]
    verbs: ["get", "update", "patch"]
  - apiGroups: ["kubeagents.x-k8s.io"]
    resources: ["agents"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: ["kubeagents.x-k8s.io"]
    resources: ["approvalrosters"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: ${AGENT_ACTOR_KSA}
  namespace: ${AGENT_NAMESPACE}
  labels:
    kube-agents/tier: developer-team
    kube-agents/role: actor
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: Role
  name: ${AGENT_ACTOR_KSA}
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: ${AGENT_ACTOR_KSA}
    namespace: ${AGENT_NAMESPACE}
